BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 461.77 Tm (The costliest failure reported is the AMA's own war story. After Dr. C Chen's practice moved to a centralized regional) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 446.55 Tm (business office, accounts receivable lagged expected volumes; digging with the practice manager revealed the office) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 431.32 Tm (was leaning on a "payer matrix" instead of obtaining prior authorizations, and denied claims were being charged off) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 416.10 Tm (with no notification. "We eventually did get the problem fixed, but not before many thousands of dollars were lost,") Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 400.87 Tm (and the only signal that caught it was the AR report [Ie5ARcGEY1g @ 00:06:29]. RCM Mastery puts hard edges on) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 385.65 Tm (the same trap: one wrong digit in a birth date is a guaranteed claim denial, and skipping pre-authorization on an MRI) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 370.42 Tm (means a 100% denial [okx8geMd-gU @ 00:01:27]. Etactics warns that attention skews toward coding, billing, denials) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 355.20 Tm (and posting, which leaves the front end that feeds them under-watched [M-Mz0m1tdrQ @ 00:00:33]. RCMOpsPro) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 339.97 Tm (closes the loop on the cascade: a patient-access failure turns into a mid-cycle coding problem and then a back-end) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 324.75 Tm (cash-flow crisis [RddJn-tA3OM @ 00:01:52]. The lesson from Chen's office is blunt. Centralizing the business office) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 309.52 Tm (did not centralize accountability, and the write-offs ran silently until someone read the receivables.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 294.30 Tm (The back-end traps are about sequence and integrity. Inlera University refuses to let anyone work AR before payment) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 279.07 Tm (posting is caught up, posts by the EFT or bank-deposit date rather than whenever convenient, and reconciles daily,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 263.85 Tm (weekly and monthly with the bookkeeper [RvLjKE1S1eU @ 00:25:58]. The same session flags charge-poster bonuses) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 248.62 Tm (as a fraud risk, sets audits at a 10% sample, and calls a modifier slapped on as a fix "abuse" [RvLjKE1S1eU @) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 233.40 Tm (01:02:44]. Victoria at Contempo Coding warns that a paid claim is not a clean bill of health: "oh well the claim got paid) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 218.17 Tm (so that must mean it's okay because the insurance paid it not necessarily," with high volumes, high-dollar claims and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 202.95 Tm (modifier 25 drawing audits that can claw money back [pG2R1NwUlro @ 00:09:31]. Greg at Amazing Charts describes) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 187.72 Tm (the owner-side version of the same blindness: physicians who do not know their own biller's software and review no) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 172.50 Tm (reports at all [eKRUeJa5ZzQ @ 00:18:21]. The common failure is trusting the payment line. Posting lag, bonus-driven) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 157.27 Tm (charge entry and modifier fixes all look like revenue until reconciliation or a payer audit shows otherwise. Inlera also) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 142.05 Tm (warns against fee schedules set at 600% of Medicare.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 126.82 Tm (The economics checks start with three numbers that PayerWatch keeps apart. Ryan O'Hara defines gross as what you) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 111.60 Tm (charge, net as what you expect and cash as what you actually get, then walks a $1 billion gross model down to $400) Tj ET
